eport. Other collectors cannot see listed prices. - Record the sale price and date with \"Mark sold\". The Sold report then shows your profit. - Arrange payment and delivery yourself, using a method that protects both sides.\n\n6. SERIAL NUMBERS AND DISPUTES\n\nOnly one unsold copy of a numbered variant can hold each serial number. If yours is already taken, the card goes to an admin. Use the \"Note for admin\" box when you add the card, because it cannot be changed later. Say how you know your number is right, for example a cert number. The full process is in the Serial-Number Dispute Procedure (serial-number-dispute-procedure.txt).\n\n7. STAYING SAFE\n\n- Ducks on the Pond Cards staff will never ask for your password, payment card number or bank details. - Keep payment and address details out of trade messages and wishlist notes. Other collectors can see public wants. - Be wary of a collector who asks you to always send first, pushes for an unusual payment method, or offers far above market value. - Report suspicious behaviour, or a card you think is counterfeit, to support.\n\n8. USING YOUR REPORTS\n\n- Summary: a quick count of what you own, what is listed, and what you have sold, with totals. - Inventory: every unsold card, with what you paid and the market price where one is known. - For sale: listed cards and the margin over what you paid. - Sold: sales, newest first, with profit.\n\nTotals include only cards with the prices filled in, so record what you pay for every card.\n\n9. COLLECTOR ETIQUETTE\n\n- Reply to offers, even with a decline. Do not leave offers hanging. - Cancel an offer you no longer want, rather than ignoring it. - Be kind in messages. Everyone was a new collector once. - Keep your Tradeable cards current. A card that is not really available wastes other collectors' time.","tokens":430,"embedding":[-0.08786395192146301,0.10459187626838684,-0.07468298822641373,-0.044126275926828384,-0.028822695836424828,0.03218429535627365,0.0725618898868560